From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Subject: Nutrition was: topics?

david@WAL6000B.UDC.UPENN.EDU (R. David Murray) said:
>What is odd is that the less I eat red meat, the less interesting I find it.

I found the same thing.  I did not get cravings for meat like I was expecting.  
I know that the less salt you eat, the less you need on your food to make it
taste salty.  I now find meat too salty and greasy, and not very tasty.

I found a similar thing when I switched from whole cow milk to soy milk.  At
first, the soy milk tasted watery and bitter.  Now that I am used to it, it
tastes good to me while the cow milk tastes greasy and thick.  I don't know
if I am just putting up with the tasted after so long, or if my perceptions
have changed.  I find my food much better tasting than it used to be.

As for "limiting" one's diet to vegetables, I am suprised at how little
variety my meat-eating friends actually have.  They eat steak, fish, chicken,
meatloaf, porkchops, and then start repeating the cycle!  I find my veggie
diet much more varied.  I wonder if it is just because I am a rebel and
will delight in being contrary to popular concepts.
