> >Consider an example: the introduction of insurance.  A market without
> >insurance is no more forced, no more distorted, and no less natural
> >than a market with insurance.  It is, however, different -- and I
> >for one find it inferior.  Inventing and marketing insurance thus
> >helps the market "work" (produce desirable outcomes), and can hardly
> >be called "getting out of the way".
> 
> 	"Getting out of the way" means not coercively interfering with
> the market process.  Setting up a new company is being part OF the market
> process.  Governments do not have the same effect on markets as businesses
> do.

  Exactly.  One big difference between private insurance and government
protection/insurance is that private insurance is actually related to real
resources present in the market whereas the government is allowed to
externalize costs and resources.  A private insurer can go bankrupt,
the government can just print more money, or it can pass a law and bring
out its armies to enforce it. If that's not a distortion, I don't know what 
is. Subsidies are probably worse. How many of our businesses are failing now
because the government decided to prop them up with tariff protection and
grants?



