Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 09:57:27 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: PPL: Intellectual property protection

Re: Patents

My understanding of the theory behind the US patent clause in
the constitution is that patents are property claims in
'idea space'.  The wording of a patent claim is analogous
to the legal description found in a property deed.  The difference
in descriptions is (a) in a property deed usually the boundary
of the property is described, and the interior area makes up the
property.  (b) in a patent claim, since boundaries are not
describable, the 'volume' in idea space is described by the
wording of the claim.  Much of the detail work in securing
a patent revolves on finicky distinctions in the claim wording,
which useds it's own specialized jargon, to cover as much of
'idea space' as possible without overlapping previous patents.

The requirement that a new patent be 'un-obvious' in difference
from a previous patent in effect creates a zone of exclusion
in 'idea space' around one patent that prevents someone else
from claiming too close to an existing patent.

'Idea space' is a multi-dimensional space mapping all possible
inventions, with one dimension for each characteristic or
component in a patent claim.  Since the number of components
in a patent is not limited, there is an unlimited number of
dimensions, and since people periodically invent new classes
of components, the size in each dimension grows.

Re: Copyrights

In the past, books were unique because they were hand-made,
and a copyright was not meaningful, since the work to make
a copy was as great as the work to write the original (minus
the thinking involved).  In the future, it will be possible
to make 'copies' individualized, and therefore traceable.

For example, take a musical recording on DAT.  It would be
possible to make minute variations in the recording (the
exact count of samples in the blank space between songs,
or a variation in the least significant bit every million
samples (roughly every 10-20 seconds).  There is enough
room to vary these parameters uniquely for each of millions
of 'copies'.  Then if duplicate tapes are made and sold,
they are traceable to who passed them on.

In a stronger case, the buyer's credit card number or
other valuble data could be encoded into the tape using
this information.  Then if a customer makes copies of his
tape to give to friends, he would also be giving away 
something valuble.  The music company could withold the
information about exactly which bits the information
was encoded into, for a year or two.  Thus the buyer
couldn't strip out the bits in making a copy, but still
be at risk later from a copy.

Dani Eder

